#use(std/code)

#section{Comments}::

Three comment forms, plus the literal-hash escape. #code{##} produces a
literal #code{#}; #code{#} followed by a space (or end of line) drops the
rest of the line; #code{#;} drops the rest of the line from that point;
#code|{#;{…}}| drops a balanced-brace span, possibly multi-line.

#code(lang: "plain")
	text ## and more
	kept #; dropped
	a #;{ b } c
	# whole line gone
	end

Live:

text ## and more
kept #; dropped
a #;{ b } c
# whole line gone
end

(The commented spans vanish; the #code{# whole line gone} line renders as an
empty line, not a paragraph break marker of its own.)

Corner cases:

#list
	#item: #code{##} works anywhere in text mode, including inside brace bodies — use it for issue ##42, hex colors, shebangs in prose.
	#item: Inside host code (#code{#(...)} or #code{(args)}) #code{#;} is Racket's #emph{datum} comment — it comments out the next s-expression, not the rest of the line — and #code{#| … |#} is the host block comment.
	#item: #code{#;} before a markup re-entry form in host code is a read error (the commented datum is read by Racket); use #code{#| … |#} there.
	#item: Inside raw bodies (code blocks, fences) #code{#} is plain text — nothing to escape.
